## 45. PVB-012 canonical laws

### PVB-012-01 — DETERMINISM

Identical canonical input SHALL produce identical canonical bytes.

### PVB-012-02 — VECTOR REPRODUCIBILITY

Canonical test vectors SHALL be independently reproducible.

### PVB-012-03 — MUTATION DETECTION

Identity-bound mutation SHALL invalidate the previous cryptographic binding.

### PVB-012-04 — DOMAIN ISOLATION

A signature SHALL NOT cross object/protocol domains.

### PVB-012-05 — FAIL CLOSED

Malformed, unsupported or ambiguous security input SHALL NOT become trusted by fallback.

### PVB-012-06 — REPLAY RESISTANCE

Replay controls SHALL supplement cryptographic verification.

### PVB-012-07 — LINEAGE PRESERVATION

Key rotation and revocation SHALL preserve historical reconstructability.

### PVB-012-08 — PROVENANCE SEPARATION

Cryptographic validity SHALL NOT substitute for provenance validation.

### PVB-012-09 — STATE HONESTY

CONFLICTED and IN_DOUBT SHALL remain explicit states.

### PVB-012-10 — CROSS-SURFACE CONTINUITY

RIO handoff SHALL preserve identity/context/provenance without creating authority.

### PVB-012-11 — CONSTITUTIONAL NEGATIVE

A valid cryptographic object SHALL NOT independently authorize constitutional action.

### PVB-012-12 — EVIDENCE

Conformance SHALL be evidence-bearing, reproducible and traceable.

